      <div class="top"><div><div class="eb">October 2025</div><h1>Secondary Market Investments</h1></div></div>
      <div class="meta4"><div><h4>Status</h4>Live (~1000 investors migrated)<br />3 Change Requests Delivered<br />Phase 2 Planned</div><div><h4>Timeline</h4>12 months (October 2025 – October 2026)</div><div><h4>Team</h4>1 Scrum Master<br />1 UX/UI Designer<br />1 DevOps Engineer<br />1 QA Engineer<br />5 Full Stack Developers<br />1 Business Analyst / Product Owner (me)</div><div><h4>Method</h4>Scrum, two-week sprints, Jira, formal change-request management</div></div>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Results at a glance</span><div class="stats"><div><b>~1,000</b><span>investors migrated</span></div><div><b>12 months</b><span>kickoff to go-live</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>change requests</span></div><div><b>10s+ → 0.8s</b><span>signing screen load</span></div><div><b>~$1,500 → &lt;$800</b><span>monthly cost</span></div></div><p class="cap">Headline results from the twelve-month engagement.</p></div>

      <section id="context"><div class="eb">Context</div><h2>The business worked. It just couldn't scale.</h2>
      <p>The client already ran a working investment business. Investors were onboarded over the phone, documents were produced by hand, and the status of every deal lived in spreadsheets and in advisors' heads.</p>
      <p>Horizon is a secondary market investment platform: investors buy units in curated fund offerings backed by private companies, and the platform runs the full lifecycle from publishing an offering to tracking returns. My brief wasn't to reinvent how investing worked. It was to turn a process people already understood into a system that could carry it, and to get a first release live without losing the control the manual process gave the business.</p>
      <div class="sub" id="problem"><div class="eb">Problem</div>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>No shared visibility in onboarding</h3><p>KYC needed actions from several people, and phone calls filled the gaps.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Deal status invisible until asked</h3><p>The path from interest to funding lived in a spreadsheet and an advisor's memory.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Every handoff a single point of failure</h3><p>One missed call or email could stall a deal.</p></div></div>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Before and after</span><div class="ba"><div class="g"><h4>Before</h4><p>Phone calls</p><p>Hand-built documents</p><p>One spreadsheet per deal</p><p>Status in advisors' heads</p></div><span>→</span><div class="p"><h4>After</h4><p>One platform for every role</p><p>Guided onboarding and KYC</p><p>Status visible at every stage</p><p>Pipeline tracked end to end</p></div></div><p class="cap">How the same deal was tracked before, on phone and spreadsheet, and now on one platform.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="principles"><div class="eb">Product principles</div><p>We didn't redesign how investing worked. Three principles, shaped with our UX designer, guided every scope call.</p>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>Same mental model</h3><p>Keep the steps, roles and sequence the business already knew.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Role clarity over feature richness</h3><p>Each role sees only what it needs to act on.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Status as the communication layer</h3><p>A handoff shows as a status that stays until someone acts, not a notification that gets missed.</p></div></div></div></section>

      <section id="built"><div class="eb">What We Built</div><h2>One pipeline, six staff roles, three investor tiers.</h2>
      <p>Every investment moves through one pipeline: Submitted → Approved → Subscribed → Allocated, with Rejected as an exit. The investor signs a subscription agreement during indication, an advisor reviews and approves it, the investor sends a wire, and a fund manager countersigns and allocates.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Investor home</span><div><img src="/images/horizon-landing.png" alt="Horizon investor dashboard — welcome banner, opportunities and venture vault" loading="lazy" style="display:block;width:100%;border-radius:2px" /></div><p class="cap">The investor home: welcome banner, opportunities and the venture vault.</p></div>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">One investment, three roles</span><div class="sc"><div class="sw"><span class="h"></span><span class="h">Submitted</span><span class="h">Approved</span><span class="h">Subscribed</span><span class="h">Allocated</span><span class="h">Investor</span><span class="a">Indicates and signs</span><span class="a">Sends wire, confirms</span><span>Waits for countersign</span><span class="a">Tracks returns</span><span class="h">Advisor</span><span class="a">Reviews and approves</span><span>Waits for payment</span><span>—</span><span>—</span><span class="h">Fund Manager</span><span>—</span><span>—</span><span class="a">Countersigns</span><span class="a">Allocates units</span></div></div><p class="cap">Who acts at each stage of one investment. Shaded cells need an action.</p></div>
