      <div><h3>Notifications</h3><p>In-app and email alerts for every role, with missed ones waiting when a user returns.</p><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Notification centre</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 28 — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The notification centre, with search and read and unread filters.</p></div></div>
      <div><h3>Fund closure</h3><p>Each offering closes first come first served, manually or automatically, as set when it is created.</p><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Closing method</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 30 — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The closing method set when an offering is created.</p></div></div>
      </div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="roles"><div class="eb">Who sees what</div><p>A privilege matrix across 14 modules defines every action each role can take. Permissions are checked live on every request, so revoking access takes effect immediately. Roles are visible in the admin UI, and changes go through a platform administrator.</p><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Access by role</span><div class="sc"><div class="am"><span></span><span>Dashboard</span><span>Offerings</span><span>Ledger</span><span>Investors</span><span>Opportunities</span><span>Users</span><span>Content</span><span>Documents</span><span>Accounts</span><span>Portfolio</span><span>Support</span><span class="l">Admin</span><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Advisor</span><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Fund Manager</span><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Affiliate</span><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Monitor</span><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Content Writer</span><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Investor · Lite</span><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><span class="l">Investor · Plus</span><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><s></s><b>Yes</b><s></s><b>Yes</b><span class="l">Investor · Pro</span><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><b>Yes</b><s></s><s></s><s></s><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b><b>Yes</b></div></div><p class="cap">Sidebar access by role. Every role also gets News &amp; Media and Settings.</p></div>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>Investors</h3><p>Lite, Plus and Pro tiers unlock more of the product step by step. Plus and Pro investors open Individual, Joint, IRA or Entity accounts.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Advisors, Affiliates, Fund Managers</h3><p>Work with assigned investors and offerings: reviewing, approving, countersigning.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Admins, Monitors, Content Writers</h3><p>Configure the platform, oversee it read-only, and publish investor content.</p></div></div></div></section>

      <section id="foundations"><div class="eb">Platform Foundations</div><h2>The team built it to last. I owned what it had to do.</h2>
      <p>Engineering built these capabilities. My part was defining the requirements and scope behind them.</p>
      <div class="sub" id="f1"><div class="eb">Signing you can defend</div><p>The in-house e-signature module is a full signing engine: a drag-and-drop template builder, multi-stage routing, and three ways to sign (draw, type or upload). It removes per-envelope vendor costs and keeps investor data inside the platform.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Signing route</span><div class="fl"><div><b>Investor</b><small>draw, type or upload</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>Joint Subscriber</b><small>joint accounts</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>Advisor</b><small>review</small></div><span>→</span><div class="p"><b>Fund Manager</b><small>countersign</small></div></div>
      <div class="chips"><span>Signed links</span><span>4-hour expiry</span><span>Single use</span><span>SHA-256 tamper detection</span><span>Consent recorded with IP and device</span><span>Immutable audit trail</span></div><p class="cap">The four-stage signing route and the safeguards on every signing link.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="f2"><div class="eb">Security &amp; access</div><p>Financial data is encrypted before it is stored, and access is checked on every request. Login runs through Cognito with role groups, and idle sessions end after 30 minutes.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Security layers</span><div class="ly"><div><b>Login</b><span>Cognito, role groups, 30-minute inactivity logout</span></div><div><b>Permissions</b><span>Fine-grained, checked live on every request</span></div><div><b>In transit</b><span>API payloads: KMS envelope encryption (AES-256-GCM)</span></div><div><b>At rest</b><span>Bank and payment fields in MongoDB encrypted at field level</span></div><div><b>Secrets</b><span>AWS SSM, pods on scoped IAM roles, no static keys</span></div></div><p class="cap">The five security layers between a user and stored financial data.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="f3"><div class="eb">Real-time &amp; reliability</div><p>Notifications are confirmed only after a live write, and offline users see what they missed. RabbitMQ runs emails, audits and scheduled jobs outside the user's request, so failed emails are retried and unsubscribe preferences are honoured. Role dashboards are cached for speed without leaking data between roles.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Notification path</span><div class="fl"><div><b>Event</b><small>action in the platform</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>RabbitMQ</b><small>retries, dead-letter queue</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>Redis Pub/Sub</b><small>works across servers</small></div><span>→</span><div class="p"><b>In-app and email</b><small>live via Server-Sent Events</small></div></div><p class="cap">The path of one notification from event to in-app and email delivery.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="f4"><div class="eb">Audit &amp; compliance</div><p>Every action is logged and kept for 3 years. Admins search the Audit Log by actor and role, and a downloadable report covers bulk user migrations.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Audit entry</span><div class="chips"><span>Actor and role</span><span>Action and status</span><span>Before and after values</span><span>3-year retention</span><span>Audit Log UI</span><span>Bulk-migration report</span></div><p class="cap">What every audit entry records, and how long it is kept.</p></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Audit Log</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 29 · Audit Log — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The Audit Log, listing each action with its actor, status and before-and-after values.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="f5"><div class="eb">Release engineering &amp; observability</div><p>Every release is immutable and traceable to a git SHA. A Linkerd service mesh gives mTLS between services, OpenTelemetry traces flow into Grafana, and Prometheus collects metrics.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Release path</span><div class="fl"><div><b>Commit</b><small>git SHA</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>Quality gate</b><small>SonarQube</small></div><span>→</span><div><b>Canary</b><small>automated success checks</small></div><span>→</span><div class="p"><b>Full rollout</b><small>immutable release</small></div></div><p class="cap">The release path from commit to full rollout.</p></div></div></section>

      <section id="decisions"><div class="eb">Key Decisions</div><h2>Five decisions that shaped the product.</h2>
      <div class="sub" id="d1"><div class="eb">Decision 01 · Investor tiers</div><h2>Tiers as a funnel: only committed investors reach live opportunities.</h2>
      <p>The business needed to tell serious investors from casual browsers without spending staff time on everyone. A person reviews the questionnaire for suitability (Lite → Plus), then verifies documents for KYC (Plus → Pro). Both gates are manual at launch; automated KYC and suitability checks are planned. Each role also gets its own dashboard analytics.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Investor tiers as a funnel</span><div class="fn"><div style="--w:100%">Lite · dashboard, news, onboarding questionnaire</div><small>Suitability review</small><div style="--w:78%">Plus · investor accounts, documents, named advisor</div><small>KYC review</small><div style="--w:56%">Pro · live opportunities, portfolio</div></div><p class="cap">The three investor tiers and the two manual reviews between them.</p></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Investor list</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 14 · Tier badges — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The investor list with tier badges and verification status.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="d2"><div class="eb">Decision 02 · Payments</div><h2>A regulated platform can't use most payment processors, yet.</h2>
      <p>Financial services sit on many processors' restricted-business lists. One declined us outright on category policy, and none of the other options we evaluated got us to launch on schedule.</p><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Payment options evaluated</span><div class="op"><div><p>Card/ACH processor<small>Declined: category policy</small></p><p>Bank-data aggregator<small>Not used for payments</small></p><p>Payment-operations platform<small>Not integrated</small></p><p>Partner bank integration<small>Not integrated</small></p></div><span>→</span><div class="w">Manual wire transfers<small>Agreed jointly with the client</small></div></div><p class="cap">The four payment options evaluated, and the route we took instead.</p></div>
      <div class="g2"><div class="c"><h3>Option A · Integrated payments</h3><p><b>Could a processor take us on?</b> One declined outright on category policy.</p><p><b>Could another option work?</b> We evaluated four. None shipped.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Option B · Manual wire transfers</h3><p><b>How does an investor pay?</b> After approval, wire instructions appear in the platform; the investor sends the transfer and confirms in the same step.</p><p><b>What do we give up?</b> Confirmation and reconciliation depend on people, not automation.</p></div></div>
      <p>The fund's bank details are entered when an admin creates the offering, and investors see them as wire instructions only after approval.</p><h3>Trading automation for launch certainty, agreed jointly with the client.</h3><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Wire instructions</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 16 · Wire instructions (blur all details) — placeholder</div><p class="cap">Step 4 of the indication flow, where wire instructions appear after approval.</p></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">The trade-off</span><div class="sl"><i><em>Where we landed</em><b></b></i><div><span>Automated reconciliation</span><span>Launch on time</span></div></div><p class="cap">The trade-off we made: automated reconciliation for launching on time.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="d3"><div class="eb">Decision 03 · E-signature</div><h2>The third-party signing screen took 10+ seconds to load.</h2>
      <p>At month 8, a cost review flagged the signing tool: slow, recurring licence and webhook fees, and investors redirected off our platform.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Signing screen load time</span><div class="bars"><div class="r g"><span>Third-party tool</span><i style="--w:100%"></i><b>10+ s</b></div><div class="r"><span>Native module</span><i style="--w:8%"></i><b>~0.8 s</b></div></div><p class="cap">Signing screen load time before and after the move to the in-house module.</p></div>
      <div class="g2"><div class="c"><h3>Option A · Keep the third-party tool</h3><p>Recurring licence and webhook fees, dependence on a vendor's uptime, pricing and API decisions, and a redirect to someone else's interface.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Option B · Build a native module</h3><p>A separate code branch kept the working integration untouched, and the build fit inside the existing two-week staging window, with testing and rollout running to the end of June. It supports templates by account type, joint co-signers, advisor review, countersignature, reminders, cancellation and an audit trail per session. It also ended per-envelope vendor costs.</p></div></div>
      <p>Investor data stays on the platform with full control over versions and audit trails, and investors stay in one branded journey. The client approved the proposal. In exchange, we own the module.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Template Builder</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 19 · Template Builder — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The Template Builder, with signature and text fields placed on a PDF.</p></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Signing sessions</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 20 · Signing sessions — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The signing sessions list, filterable by status and signing stage.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="d4"><div class="eb">Decision 04 · Infrastructure &amp; cost</div><h2>A month-8 cost review turned an overrun into an optimisation.</h2>
      <p>Early provisioning assumptions between us and the client weren't fully aligned, and cloud spend ran above plan. The review brought monthly cost from about $1,500 to under $800.</p>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Monthly operating cost</span><div class="bars"><div class="r g"><span>Before review</span><i style="--w:100%"></i><b>~$1,500</b></div><div class="r"><span>After review</span><i style="--w:53%"></i><b>&lt;$800</b></div></div><p class="cap">Monthly operating cost before and after the month-8 review.</p></div>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">Environment moves</span><div class="bars"><div class="r g"><span>CR01 · Staging</span><i style="--w:100%"></i><b>80 h</b></div><div class="r"><span>CR02 · Production</span><i style="--w:70%"></i><b>56 h</b></div></div><p class="cap">Hours for the staging and production environment moves.</p></div>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>Managed Kubernetes</h3><p>Staging moved from single-host containers to managed Kubernetes (CR01, two weeks).</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Reuse paid off</h3><p>Production (CR02) took one week, about 30% fewer hours, by reusing the staging work.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Visible scope</h3><p>The client has no technical team, so environment setup became two change requests, not hidden scope.</p></div></div></div>
      <div class="sub" id="d5"><div class="eb">Decision 05 · Scope</div><h2>When the full integration didn't fit, we kept the smallest useful piece.</h2>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>Bank connection, read-only</h3><p>Admins link the fund's bank when creating an offering, so verified details reach investors, and balances and transactions are retrieved to check incoming wires. Investors never link a bank.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>User migration from CSV</h3><p>Investor records came across from CRM exports, and about 1,000 investors were migrated.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Documents deferred</h3><p>Document migration moved to Phase 2, for when the client is ready.</p></div></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Bank Details tab</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 23 · Bank Details tab — placeholder</div><p class="cap">The Bank Details tab in offering creation, where the fund's bank is connected.</p></div></div></section>

      <section id="delivery"><div class="eb">Delivery Story</div><h2>Delivering through a team change and a client-side delay.</h2><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Twelve-month timeline</span><div class="sc"><div class="gt"><b></b><span class="m">Oct</span><span class="m">Nov</span><span class="m">Dec</span><span class="m">Jan</span><span class="m">Feb</span><span class="m">Mar</span><span class="m">Apr</span><span class="m">May</span><span class="m">Jun</span><span class="m">Jul</span><span class="m">Aug</span><span class="m">Sep</span><span class="m">Oct</span><b>Core build · Sprints 4–13</b><i data-t="Dec – Apr" style="grid-column:4/9">Product modules</i><b>Environments</b><i data-t="Apr – Jul" style="grid-column:8/12">UAT, then production</i><b>E-signature module</b><i data-t="Late May – Jun" style="grid-column:9/11">Build and test</i><b>Payment options</b><i data-t="May – Jun" style="grid-column:9/11">Setup and research</i><b>Migration &amp; closure</b><i data-t="Jul – early Aug" style="grid-column:11/13">Users, fund closure</i><b>Hold &amp; rebrand</b><i data-t="Aug – Oct" style="grid-column:12/15">Rebrand, CR03</i><b>Go-live</b><i class="g" data-t="Oct" style="grid-column:14/15">Go-live</i></div></div><div class="chips"><span>Nov · a backend developer joins</span><span>Jan · leaves</span><span>Feb · two developers join</span><span>Apr · QA engineer joins</span></div><p class="cap">From kickoff in October 2025 to production go-live in October 2026. Team changes are listed below the bars.</p></div>
      <div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>A team change</h3><p>A backend developer who joined in November left in January without a knowledge transfer, which left some technical debt. We worked with one backend developer for about a month until two developers joined in February. The scrum master spread the work across the whole team, himself included.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>A client-side delay</h3><p>The client held deployment in mid-August for reasons on their side. We used the window to confirm the brand direction and complete the rebrand change request before go-live in October.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>CR03: more than a rebrand</h3><p>About two-thirds of its 320 hours went to the rebrand, domain changes and UI refresh. The rest was unit batches with FIFO allocation, cost basis and non-compounding returns, plus the bank connection. Tracking units bought at different prices is the clearest secondary-market signal.</p></div></div>
      <div class="fg"><span class="lb">CR03 scope</span><div class="bars"><div class="r"><span>Brand &amp; UI</span><i style="--w:100%"></i><b>125 h</b></div><div class="r"><span>Infra, domain, QA, DevOps</span><i style="--w:66%"></i><b>83 h</b></div><div class="r g"><span>Unit batches &amp; returns</span><i style="--w:45%"></i><b>56 h</b></div><div class="r g"><span>Bank connection</span><i style="--w:45%"></i><b>56 h</b></div></div><p class="cap">How CR03's 320 hours split between the rebrand and new capability.</p></div></section>

      <section id="quality"><div class="eb">Quality &amp; Ways of Working</div><h2>Scrum, Jira and a QA engineer from April.</h2>
      <div class="g2"><div class="c"><h3>Cadence</h3><p>Two-week sprints, with backlog refinement alongside the client.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>One source of truth</h3><p>Jira for workflows, bugs and delivery tracking.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Change control</h3><p>Three change requests, each scoped and estimated in writing before work started.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Testing</h3><p>Developers tested through the build. A QA engineer joined in April, ahead of the UAT environment.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Quality gates</h3><p>SonarQube gates on the code, and canary releases with automated success checks before full rollout.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Knowledge base</h3><p>Requirements, a version-controlled user manual, and source material for a planned assistant.</p></div></div><div class="fg"><span class="lb">Sprint board</span><div class="ph">Screenshot 27 · Jira board (masked) — placeholder</div><p class="cap">A sprint board in Jira, with names and ticket titles masked.</p></div></section>

      <section id="outcomes"><div class="eb">Outcomes</div><h2>The client launched with about 1,000 investors migrated, and kept coming back with change requests.</h2>
      <blockquote>"This is exactly what we talked about wanting—all those little things here and there where you think, 'I wish they had this, I wish they had that.'"<span>Client leadership</span></blockquote></section>

      <section id="learned"><div class="eb">Retrospective</div><h2>What I learned</h2><div class="g2">
      <div><h3>Test external dependencies in discovery, not mid-build.</h3><p>Financial services sit on many payment processors' restricted lists, and we only learned that after designing around one. Dropping to manual wires and keeping a read-only bank connection got us to launch, but an early eligibility check would have saved weeks of rework.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Write handovers and assumptions down as deliverables.</h3><p>A developer left without a knowledge transfer, and early cloud sizing assumptions were never signed off. Both cost us time and money. I'd now treat a handover checklist and an approved environment-sizing note as part of the scope.</p></div></div></section>

      <section id="next"><div class="eb">What's Next</div><h2>Phase 2</h2><div class="g3"><div class="c"><h3>Document migration &amp; enhancements</h3><p>Bring existing documents into the platform and keep refining what's live.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Automated KYC &amp; suitability</h3><p>Replace the manual gates with automated checks.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Mobile app &amp; knowledge assistant</h3><p>A mobile app, and an internal assistant, an idea the client raised, to query their own knowledge sources.</p></div></div>
      <p style="margin-top:28px;font-style:italic;font-size:12px">Horizon is an alias. Client, vendor and product names are withheld by agreement.</p></section>

      <section style="padding-top:48px"><div class="eb" style="font-size:15px;color:var(--tx)">Next case studies</div><br />
